2-permissions/3-capabilities-and-sharing.description:
A capability is one named thing an app may ask a worker to do. Apps are granted capabilities, not workers - {app -&gt; {capability: level}}, stored encrypted and checked on every call. A capability that was never granted is denied before the request leaves the broker.A capability is a plain name, such as GetEvents or AddEvent. The Manager routes a call to the first worker that announces that name, so each name must be unique across the fleet. Worker health probes are the exception: they carry the worker's name (Calendar.Ping, Alarm.Ping) so that each one can be reached on its own.Events are granted with an Events. prefix: Events.AlarmFired, Events.ThemeChanged.Each worker chooses a curated surface: the cross-program calls, not every method of its class. All 28 workers expose real capabilities now (140 in total). The Calendar worker carries the most, and shows the pattern:The full list for every worker is in <a data-href="5 - Permission Catalog" href="2-permissions/5-permission-catalog.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">5 - Permission Catalog</a>."Event stuff shared with different programs" means this: the Calendar worker owns the calendar. Any program the Manager grants GetEvents and AddEvent reads and writes the same calendar through it. Two apps, one shared store, each held to its own grant.<br>The demo is the Höllvania Municipal Calendar, a Warframe-1999-flavoured XRUIOS.SampleApp built with <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="https://spectreconsole.net" rel="noopener nofollow" class="external-link is-unresolved" href="https://spectreconsole.net" target="_self">Spectre.Console</a>. It is granted GetEvents and AddEvent but not DeleteEvent, so it shows both sides: a call that works and a call that is refused. See <a data-href="7 - Proven at Runtime" href="2-permissions/7-proven-at-runtime.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">7 - Proven at Runtime</a>.using XRUIOS.Interfaces; // credentials: from the environment, a creds file, or enrollment (see "A CMD App")
var mgr = await EclipseSecureClient.ConnectAsync(brokerAddr, "sample-app", appPsk, identity: appId); // granted: write an event, then read the day back (another granted app would see this event too)
string uid = await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("AddEvent", new() { ["day"] = today, ["summary"] = "Ship XRUIOS" });
string events = await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("GetEvents", new() { ["day"] = today }); // NOT granted: the broker refuses before it reaches the worker
try { await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("DeleteEvent", new() { ["uid"] = uid }); }
catch { /* ACCESS DENIED BY THE OPERATOR */ }
On the Manager side, the grants are the app's entry in AppCatalog.cs:new("sampleapp", new[] { "XRUIOS.SampleApp" }, new[] { "GetEvents", "AddEvent" }, Array.Empty&lt;string&gt;()),
// DeleteEvent is deliberately left out -&gt; the broker denies it
At each start the Manager registers the app (a fresh password every run) and grants exactly the listed names. Remove a name from the list and the next start revokes it. Grant a second app the same two names and it sees the first app's event: shared, but each still walled to what it was given.<br>A third-party app does the same with a manifest file and XRUIOS.Manager install-app; see <a data-href="8 - Building a XRUIOS App" href="2-permissions/8-building-a-xruios-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">8 - Building a XRUIOS App</a>.<br>Next: getting it running - <a data-href="4 - Installing" href="2-permissions/4-installing.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">4 - Installing</a>.

2-permissions/8-building-a-xruios-app.ht.description:
An "app" on XRUIOS is anything that holds a stable id and its own app password (PSK) and talks to the Manager broker over the Eclipse channel. It never touches a worker, a file, or a key directly: it asks the broker for a capability by name, and the broker either runs it in the owning worker or refuses it. That contract is identical whether your app is a console tool, a desktop window, a game-engine shell, or a mod inside a game engine. Only the shell around the client changes.This page is a set of complete examples, smallest first. Each one comes with the manifest that grants it what it needs. The projects behind them are on <a data-href="3 - A CMD App" href="3-building/3-a-cmd-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">3 - A CMD App</a>, <a data-href="4 - A Desktop App" href="3-building/4-a-desktop-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">4 - A Desktop App</a> and <a data-href="5 - An Aether Mod" href="3-building/5-an-aether-mod.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">5 - An Aether Mod</a>.your app ──(app id + app password)──► Manager broker │ post-quantum handshake (Kyber + AES-256-GCM) │ XRUIOS.Permission check on the capability name ▼ owning worker runs it, result comes back
An app needs three things, and it invents none of them:
An id the Manager gives it. It is always app:&lt;name&gt;, where &lt;name&gt; is the name in the app's manifest.
Its password (PSK). The Manager makes a new one for every app each time it starts, and keeps it in memory.
The broker address, the loopback port the Manager is listening on.
The Manager hands these over in one of three ways, and the app uses the first that is available:So an app cannot choose where to connect, cannot invent a password, and cannot pretend to be another app. A copy of the executable on another machine, or in another folder, gets nothing.The console examples all start the same way. This small file finds the credentials and connects. (It is the same ladder WorkerBroker uses in the desktop apps, without the creds file.)using XRUIOS.Interfaces; public static class Xruios
{ public static async Task&lt;AppProvisioning&gt; CredentialsAsync(string appName) { string? id = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_ID"); string? psk = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_PSK"); string? addr = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_BROKER_ADDR"); if (id != null &amp;&amp; psk != null &amp;&amp; addr != null) return new AppProvisioning { AppId = id, PskBase64 = psk, BrokerAddress = addr }; // Throws "No XRUIOS.Manager is running to enroll with." if there is no unlocked Manager. return await XruiosEnrollment.EnrollAsync(appName); } public static Task&lt;EclipseSecureClient&gt; ConnectAsync(AppProvisioning p, string clientName) =&gt; EclipseSecureClient.ConnectAsync(p.BrokerAddress, clientName, p.Psk, identity: p.AppId);
}
<br>The .csproj for every console example is the one on <a data-href="3 - A CMD App" href="3-building/3-a-cmd-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">3 - A CMD App</a>: a net10.0 exe that references XRUIOS.SecureLayer and EclipseProject.dll.The smallest useful app. It reads today's events and adds one.var cred = await Xruios.CredentialsAsync("agenda");
await using var mgr = await Xruios.ConnectAsync(cred, "agenda"); string today = DateTime.Today.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd"); string uid = await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("AddEvent", new() { ["day"] = today, ["summary"] = "Stand-up" });
Console.WriteLine($"added {uid}"); string events = await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("GetEvents", new() { ["day"] = today });
foreach (var row in events.Split(';', StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries)) Console.WriteLine(row.Trim());
Its manifest, agenda.json:{ "name": "agenda", "exe": "C:\\Tools\\Agenda\\Agenda.exe", "capabilities": [ "GetEvents", "AddEvent" ], "events": []
}
XRUIOS.Manager install-app agenda.json
XRUIOS.Manager stop
XRUIOS.Manager start
Each row GetEvents returns is the start time and the summary, for example 09:00 Stand-up. An event with no time of day shows 00:00.GetEvents and AddEvent also take an optional calendarId. Call ListCalendars (add it to capabilities) to find the ids; it returns id|name|color;....Capabilities return plain text, so an app parses it. This one reads the clock and runs a stopwatch with laps.var cred = await Xruios.CredentialsAsync("laptimer");
await using var mgr = await Xruios.ConnectAsync(cred, "laptimer"); // "shortTime|shortDate|longTime|longDate|zone|iso"
var now = (await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("Now", new() { ["tz"] = "" })).Split('|');
Console.WriteLine($"{now[0]} on {now[1]} ({now[4]})"); string id = await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("StopwatchStart"); // returns the stopwatch id
Console.WriteLine("Press Enter for a lap, q then Enter to stop.");
while (Console.ReadLine() != "q")
{ // "lapNumber|secondsElapsed" var lap = (await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("StopwatchLap", new() { ["id"] = id })).Split('|'); Console.WriteLine($"lap {lap[0]} at {lap[1]} s");
} // "hh:mm:ss.fff;1|12;2|30": the final time, then each lap
var parts = (await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("StopwatchStop", new() { ["id"] = id })).Split(';');
Console.WriteLine($"total {parts[0]}");
{ "name": "laptimer", "exe": "C:\\Tools\\LapTimer\\LapTimer.exe", "capabilities": [ "Now", "StopwatchStart", "StopwatchLap", "StopwatchStop" ], "events": []
}
If the app calls StopwatchElapsed it must be added to the list too. Asking for a stopwatch id that does not exist comes back as an ordinary failed call carrying the worker's own message ("A Stopwatch with this ID does not exist."), so catch it and show it.An app that reacts when something happens does not poll. It opens an event stream. The stream keeps its own session, reconnects by itself, and picks up from where it left off after a blip.using XRUIOS.Interfaces;
using XRUIOS.Interpreter; var cred = await Xruios.CredentialsAsync("alarmwatch"); await using var stream = XruiosEventStream.Start(cred.BrokerAddress, cred.AppId, cred.Psk, e =&gt; { if (e.Name != XruiosEventNames.AlarmFired) return; var alarm = EventJson.Read&lt;AlarmInfo&gt;(e.Payload); Console.WriteLine($"ALARM '{alarm.Name}' at {alarm.Time}"); }, onError: ex =&gt; Console.Error.WriteLine(ex.Message)); Console.WriteLine("Watching. Press Enter to quit.");
Console.ReadLine();
{ "name": "alarmwatch", "exe": "C:\\Tools\\AlarmWatch\\AlarmWatch.exe", "capabilities": [], "events": [ "AlarmFired" ]
}
An event is delivered only if the app holds Events.&lt;Name&gt;. The manifest's events list is turned into those grants. An app with an empty list connects fine and simply never sees anything. A handler that throws is reported through onError and does not stop the stream.A desktop app is Example 1 with a window on top. The apps that ship use OpenSilver in a Photino window, and a page is as short as this:Loaded += async (_, _) =&gt;
{ await AppShell.InitializeAsync("XRUIOS.App.Time"); ActiveCountLine.Text = await TimeSource.NowAsync();
};
<br>TimeSource.NowAsync() is a hole: a one-file wrapper around the Now capability. The whole walkthrough, including a WPF version, is on <a data-href="4 - A Desktop App" href="3-building/4-a-desktop-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">4 - A Desktop App</a>.PocketPal, the assistant, does not choose a model. Any app can provide the answers. When an app calls Ask, the Assistant worker stores the question, announces AssistantAsked, and waits (up to 15 seconds by default) for a provider to call AnswerAssistant. With no provider, the question stays pending and shows unanswered in the history.You write the brain; AssistantProvider handles the plumbing.using XRUIOS.Apps.Shared;
using XRUIOS.Interpreter; public sealed class EchoBrain : IAssistantInterpreter
{ // Replace with a local model, a remote API, a rules engine... public Task&lt;string&gt; AskAsync(string prompt, CancellationToken ct) =&gt; Task.FromResult($"You asked: {prompt}");
} await AppShell.InitializeAsync("mybrain");
await using var pal = await AssistantProvider.StartAsync( AppShell.Broker, new EchoBrain(), ex =&gt; Console.Error.WriteLine(ex.Message));
Console.ReadLine();
{ "name": "mybrain", "exe": "C:\\Tools\\MyBrain\\MyBrain.exe", "capabilities": [ "AnswerAssistant", "PendingQuestions" ], "events": [ "AssistantAsked" ]
}
Questions are answered one at a time. Questions asked while no provider was running are fetched with PendingQuestions when the provider starts. AssistantProvider is in XRUIOS.Apps.Shared, so a provider references that project, as the UI apps do. The app that asked also learns of a late answer through the AssistantAnswered event.An engine can be the XRUIOS shell. It connects as a normal app, receives the events it was granted, and draws things itself instead of the desktop doing it. Reference XRUIOS.Aether (it targets net8.0 for Godot), then:using XRUIOS.Aether;
using XRUIOS.Interpreter; public partial class XruiosBridge : Node
{ private AetherShell? _shell; public override async void _Ready() { // Your own presenter for notifications (an in-world panel, say). XruiosInterpreter.Use&lt;INotificationInterpreter&gt;(new WorldPanelNotifier(this)); _shell = await AetherShell.ConnectAsync( "MyAetherShell", post: a =&gt; Callable.From(a).CallDeferred(), // Godot's scene tree is main-thread only onError: ex =&gt; GD.PrintErr(ex.Message)); GD.Print(await _shell.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("Now")); } public override void _ExitTree() =&gt; _ = _shell?.DisposeAsync();
} public sealed class WorldPanelNotifier : INotificationInterpreter
{ private readonly Node _host; public WorldPanelNotifier(Node host) =&gt; _host = host; public void Show(string title, string body, string? source = null) { /* spawn a panel */ }
}
{ "name": "MyAetherShell", "exe": "C:\\Games\\MyShell\\MyShell.exe", "capabilities": [ "Now" ], "events": [ "ShellNotify", "ShellAppearance", "ShellLaunch", "NotificationPosted" ]
}
Two more things make this work:
Start the Manager with XRUIOS_SHELL=aether in its environment. The workers it launches inherit it, and from then on the visual parts of a worker (toasts, dark mode and wallpaper, opening a scene) are sent to the engine as ShellNotify, ShellAppearance and ShellLaunch events instead of being done on the desktop. The rest (audio, power, clipboard) stays on the OS. App ids that start with aether: are scenes.
The engine's exe in the manifest is the engine executable. It enrolls like any other app, so its folder is approved with install-app first.
<br>AetherShell hands each event to the interpreter you registered for it. Events no interpreter handled arrive on its Unhandled event. More in <a data-href="15 - XRUIOS.Aether and Apps.Shared" href="4-understanding-the-wiring/15-xruios.aether-and-apps.shared.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">15 - XRUIOS.Aether and Apps.Shared</a> and <a data-href="13 - XRUIOS.Interpreter" href="4-understanding-the-wiring/13-xruios.interpreter.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">13 - XRUIOS.Interpreter</a>.Edit the manifest and install it again. The grants follow the file, and a capability you removed is revoked the next time the Manager starts.XRUIOS.Manager apps # every app, its exe, capabilities and events
XRUIOS.Manager install-app agenda.json # add or change
XRUIOS.Manager uninstall-app agenda # remove, revoke its grants, forget its approval
Rebuilding the app changes its files, so approve it again: XRUIOS.Manager baseline agenda. Until you do, enrollment is refused with "app failed integrity check".<br>A mod is an app that runs inside a host (a HangarBay / Aether game or scene) instead of as its own process, so it can't even open a socket to the broker: CasCore made System.Net uncallable. Instead the host holds the permission authority and the mod asks the host. The permission story is the same - one XRUIOS.Permission authority, capabilities by name - but the plumbing is the mod registry, not EclipseSecureClient. Full detail in <a data-href="6 - Aether Engine Integration" href="2-permissions/6-aether-engine-integration.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">6 - Aether Engine Integration</a>; here is the build.<br>A note on names: the mod registry and its test (XRUIOS.AetherModTest) use their own capability strings, such as Time.Calendar:GetEvents, in the host's permission store. Those belong to the host's policy. The Manager's broker, used by every other example on this page, uses the plain names from <a data-href="5 - Permission Catalog" href="2-permissions/5-permission-catalog.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">5 - Permission Catalog</a>.On the host side (the game integrating XRUIOS - written once, not by the mod author):// one registry over the host's real XRUIOS.Permissions handler + context
var mods = new ModRegistry(handler, appContext, privateRoot, memoTtl: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // enroll the mod by its Notary-verified package id -&gt; host-assigned ModId
var widget = mods.Enroll("com.hollvania.calendarwidget"); // grant it exactly what it may do - persisted in XRUIOS.Permissions under its ModId
await mods.GrantAsync(widget, "Time.Calendar:GetEvents");
// note: no AddEvent, no DeleteEvent granted // give it a jailed filesystem: its own private dir, plus any named roots it's granted
var fs = mods.CreateFileSystem(widget, grantableRoots);
On the mod side, the author writes against what the host hands in - never against System.IO, System.Net, or the broker. Two real doors exist today (both are in the Hangarbay engine, not made up here):using Walker.Content.Contracts;
using Walker.Content.Security; // A scene mod: the host calls these, the mod never runs its own loop.
public class CalendarWidget : IHostedScene
{ private IHostServices _host; public void OnLaunch(IHostServices host) { _host = host; host.Log("widget up: " + host.PackageId); } public void Tick(double delta) { /* draw, using host.Resources for textures and audio */ } public void OnSuspend() { } public void OnResume() { } public void OnShutdown() { }
} // Files go through the jailed file system the host builds for this mod.
public string SaveNote(SandboxFileSystem fs)
{ fs.WriteAllText("notes/ok.txt", "hello from mod"); // needs fs.write return fs.ReadAllText("notes/ok.txt"); // needs fs.read
}
IHostServices offers the package id, the resource catalog, spawning models, signals and logging. It does not offer XRUIOS capabilities such as the calendar. To give a mod one, the host writes a small service of its own: it holds a normal app connection (Example 6), and before every call it asks registry.IsAllowed(mod, "Time.Calendar:GetEvents") and only then calls the broker. That bridge is not shipped yet. Anything the mod tries outside these doors (File, Process, sockets) is blocked by CasCore whatever it was granted.<br>The mod author's mental model is the same contract, minus the connection: ask the host for something, get a result or a refusal. What changes is that identity is host-assigned (ModId from the Notary package id, so a mod can't claim to be another), grants are persisted per ModId, sensors and files are themselves capabilities, and an unsigned mod (EnrollEphemeral) can't bank trust across sessions. The runtime proof is in <a data-href="6 - Aether Engine Integration" href="2-permissions/6-aether-engine-integration.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">6 - Aether Engine Integration</a>.<br>A worker owns its store and sets it up itself on first launch; the Manager does not reach into a worker's data. By the time the broker routes your first AddEvent, the Calendar worker has already created its Calendar directory. An app should never have to initialize a worker it cannot even see. When you add a worker, put its setup in its own Program.cs (see <a data-href="2 - A Worker" href="3-building/2-a-worker.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">2 - A Worker</a>).<br>Your app's surface is the set of names it was granted. Design the app around the names you will call, then list exactly those in the manifest. The full list is in <a data-href="5 - Permission Catalog" href="2-permissions/5-permission-catalog.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">5 - Permission Catalog</a>. Anything else is denied by construction.A denial and a handshake failure are the security model doing its job. Treat them as expected states, not exceptions to bury.Console, desktop, engine shell or mod: your app is not trusted with a secret it can leak. The Manager makes the password fresh on every start, gives it only to the process that passed its checks, and keeps it in memory. The mod never has one at all, because its host connects. This is what makes "just send me the exe" safe.<br>Related: <a data-href="1 - The Model" href="2-permissions/1-the-model.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">1 - The Model</a> · <a data-href="3 - Capabilities and Sharing" href="2-permissions/3-capabilities-and-sharing.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">3 - Capabilities and Sharing</a> · <a data-href="6 - Aether Engine Integration" href="2-permissions/6-aether-engine-integration.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">6 - Aether Engine Integration</a> · <a data-href="7 - Proven at Runtime" href="2-permissions/7-proven-at-runtime.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">7 - Proven at Runtime</a> · <a data-href="14 - The Chain" href="4-understanding-the-wiring/14-the-chain.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">14 - The Chain</a>

3-building/2-a-worker.html.description:
A worker is one system class running in its own process, exposing a curated set of capabilities the Manager can route to. This is where a new class from Barebones becomes a first-class part of the fleet. Every worker follows the shape of the Chrono worker, which is small enough to read in one sitting.A worker is three files plus its system source:src/workers/XRUIOS.Worker.&lt;Class&gt;/
├─ &lt;Class&gt;.csproj compiles ONLY this class's source
├─ Program.cs boot: bind context, init, run the secured host
└─ Capabilities.cs the [SeaOfDirac] curated surface src/systems/&lt;Class&gt;Class.cs the class itself, written once and linked in
The isolation is the point: the worker &lt;Compile Include&gt;s just its own class file, so a breached Songs worker doesn't even contain Calendar's code. See <a data-href="1 - The Model" href="2-permissions/1-the-model.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">1 - The Model</a>.This is the real Chrono project, whole:&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web"&gt; &lt;PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net10.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt; &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt; &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt; &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt; &lt;RootNamespace&gt;XRUIOS.Worker.Chrono&lt;/RootNamespace&gt; &lt;AssemblyName&gt;XRUIOS.Worker.Chrono&lt;/AssemblyName&gt; &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;Compile Include="../../systems/ChronoClass.cs" /&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt; &lt;/Project&gt;
Two things to notice:
Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web, not the plain SDK: a worker hosts a MagicOnion/gRPC endpoint the Manager connects to.
No references here. Everything a worker needs is in src/workers/Directory.Build.props, which every worker under src/workers/ inherits automatically:
If your class needs a package no other class does, add it to that class's own .csproj rather than the shared props. Keep the shared set to what every class needs.Capabilities are methods marked [SeaOfDirac]. WorkerOcean scans the assembly for them, and the Manager routes a call by the capability name. Keep the surface curated: the cross-program calls, not every public method. This is the real Now capability from Chrono:using EclipseProject;
using XRUIOS.Barebones; namespace XRUIOS.Worker.Chrono
{ public static class ChronoCapabilities { [SeaOfDirac("Chrono.Ping", new[] { "input" }, typeof(string), typeof(string))] public static string Ping(string input) =&gt; "XRUIOS.Worker.Chrono: " + input; [SeaOfDirac("Now", new[] { "tz" }, typeof(string), typeof(string))] public static string Now(string tz = "") { /* ... */ } }
}
The attribute is [SeaOfDirac(name, argumentNames, returnType, argumentTypes...)]. A few rules that come from how WorkerOcean works:
Argument names are bound by name. The caller sends { "tz": "..." }, and the value goes to the parameter called tz. A parameter with a C# default value may be left out, so you can add an optional argument later without breaking existing callers.
Integers travel as long. Declare a count as long in the attribute and the method, not int.
Async is supported. Return Task or Task&lt;T&gt;.
Return plain strings (or simple DTOs). Lists use ; between rows and | between fields, so any client can read them without sharing your internal types.
Throwing is fine. The message reaches the app as a failed call. Use it for bad input, for example throw new ArgumentException("Ask needs a prompt.").
Names must be unique across the fleet. The broker routes to the first worker that announces a name. Every worker also exposes &lt;Worker&gt;.Ping so it can be probed on its own.
This is the real Chrono Program.cs. Every worker boots the same way: take the context the Manager handed it, do its own one-time setup, then run the secured host.using System.Reflection;
using XRUIOS.Interfaces; // Take the Manager's context (paths and this worker's own key) from the environment.
global::XRUIOS.Barebones.XRUIOS.BindFromEnvironment(); // This worker's OWN setup. The Manager never reaches into a worker's data.
if (File.Exists(Path.Combine(global::XRUIOS.Barebones.XRUIOS.DataPath, "Chrono", "Chrono.json"))) await global::XRUIOS.Barebones.ChronoClass.LoadDateData(); const string ServerName = "XRUIOS.Worker.Chrono"; await SecureWorkerHost.Run( serverName: ServerName, capabilityAssembly: Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly());
<br>There is no gate argument and no guard argument. Run uses ManagerOnlyGate and builds the NotaryGuard for the worker's own folder. It also hardens the process, checks the Manager-signed baseline, opens the audit log, and publishes the worker's address. The full order is in <a data-href="5 - XRUIOS.Worker" href="4-understanding-the-wiring/5-xruios.worker.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">5 - XRUIOS.Worker</a>. Do not pass AllowAllPermissionGate; it is obsolete.This example adds a made-up Greeter worker with one capability.1. The system class (src/systems/GreeterClass.cs). Plain code with no XRUIOS types:namespace XRUIOS.Barebones
{ public static class GreeterClass { public static string Greet(string name, string language) =&gt; language == "fr" ? $"Bonjour, {name}" : $"Hello, {name}"; }
}
2. The project (src/workers/XRUIOS.Worker.Greeter/Greeter.csproj): copy Chrono's project and change the names and the Compile Include to ../../systems/GreeterClass.cs.3. The capabilities (Capabilities.cs):using EclipseProject;
using XRUIOS.Barebones;
using XRUIOS.Interfaces; namespace XRUIOS.Worker.Greeter
{ public static class GreeterCapabilities { [SeaOfDirac("Greeter.Ping", new[] { "input" }, typeof(string), typeof(string))] public static string Ping(string input) =&gt; "XRUIOS.Worker.Greeter: " + input; [SeaOfDirac("Greet", new[] { "name", "language" }, typeof(string), typeof(string), typeof(string))] public static string Greet(string name, string language = "en") { // Who asked? Set by the Manager, so the app cannot fake it. string who = WorkerCall.CallerAppId ?? "unknown"; WorkerEvents.Publish("GreetingMade", $"{{\"for\":\"{name}\",\"by\":\"{who}\"}}"); return GreeterClass.Greet(name, language); } }
}
4. The boot (Program.cs): the same as Chrono's, with ServerName = "XRUIOS.Worker.Greeter". Drop the LoadDateData lines unless your class needs setup.5. Register it with the Manager. Two edits outside the worker:
Add ("Greeter", PermissionGroup.Interface) to WorkerCatalog.Defs in XRUIOS.Manager/WorkerCatalog.cs, so the Manager finds, launches and supervises it.
Add the project to XRUIOS.slnx.
6. Build, approve, restart.dotnet build src/workers/XRUIOS.Worker.Greeter
XRUIOS.Manager baseline Greeter
XRUIOS.Manager stop
XRUIOS.Manager start
The baseline step is not optional. The Manager will not launch a folder that has no signed baseline, and the worker will not run without one.7. Grant it to an app. Add "Greet" to that app's capability list, in AppCatalog.cs for a built-in app or in the manifest of a third-party app. Add "GreetingMade" to the app's events if it should receive the event (the grant is Events.GreetingMade). Ungranted names are denied at the broker before they reach the worker.<br>8. Check it. Add Greeter to the list of workers in XRUIOS.Diagnostics so it gets a ping probe, then run the harness (see <a data-href="6 - The Diagnostics Harness" href="3-building/6-the-diagnostics-harness.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">6 - The Diagnostics Harness</a>).<br>Next: the client that calls it - <a data-href="3 - A CMD App" href="3-building/3-a-cmd-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">3 - A CMD App</a>.

3-building/3-a-cmd-app.html.description:
The smallest thing that talks to XRUIOS: a console program that connects to the Manager broker with its own credentials and calls capabilities by name. This is the reference shell - XRUIOS.SampleApp (the Höllvania calendar) is exactly this, and every other client is a variation on it.&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt; &lt;PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt; &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net10.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt; &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt; &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt; &lt;RootNamespace&gt;XRUIOS.MyApp&lt;/RootNamespace&gt; &lt;AssemblyName&gt;XRUIOS.MyApp&lt;/AssemblyName&gt; &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\XRUIOS.SecureLayer\XRUIOS.SecureLayer.csproj" /&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="Spectre.Console" Version="0.49.1" /&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;Reference Include="EclipseProject"&gt;&lt;HintPath&gt;..\..\libs\EclipseProject.dll&lt;/HintPath&gt;&lt;/Reference&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt; &lt;/Project&gt;
Why each reference:That's the entire dependency set. An app needs nothing from the system classes, no keys, no Pariah - it only knows how to ask the broker.An app needs three things, none of which it invents: its id, its PSK, and the broker address. The Manager provisions them. Look for them in this order:// 1. environment handoff (the Manager can launch you with these set)
string? appId = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_ID");
string? pskB64 = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_PSK");
string? brokerAddr = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_BROKER_ADDR"); // 2. a creds file the Manager wrote (dev convenience)
if (appId is null || pskB64 is null || brokerAddr is null) (appId, pskB64, brokerAddr) = TryCredsFile("myapp"); // %LocalAppData%\XRUIOS\Public\myapp.creds // 3. self-enroll against the running Manager
if (appId is null || pskB64 is null || brokerAddr is null)
{ var prov = await XruiosEnrollment.EnrollAsync("myapp"); appId = prov.AppId; pskB64 = prov.PskBase64; brokerAddr = prov.BrokerAddress;
} byte[] appPsk = Convert.FromBase64String(pskB64!);
The PSK never lives in your binary. Copy the exe to another machine and it authenticates as nobody, because the id and PSK were minted by this machine's Manager. That's what makes "just send me the exe" safe.What each of the three ways needs from the Manager:If enrollment is refused you get a message such as "caller is not the registered app (path mismatch)" or "app failed integrity check". Both mean the same thing: rebuild changed the folder, so approve it again.using XRUIOS.Interfaces; // brings SecureLayer's client types // post-quantum handshake (Kyber + AES-256-GCM). identity: is your stable app id.
var mgr = await EclipseSecureClient.ConnectAsync(brokerAddr!, "myapp-client", appPsk, identity: appId!); // a granted capability runs in the owning worker and returns its result
string uid = await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("AddEvent", new Dictionary&lt;string, object?&gt; { ["day"] = "2026-08-21", ["summary"] = "Ship it" }); // an ungranted capability is refused at the broker before it reaches a worker
try { await mgr.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("DeleteEvent", new() { ["uid"] = uid }); }
catch (Exception ex) { /* XRUIOS.Permission denied 'DeleteEvent' for app:myapp */ } await mgr.DisposeAsync();
InvokeAsync&lt;T&gt; packs the args, sends them over the encrypted channel, and deserializes the reply to T. The capability name is the whole API - what you can call is exactly what the Manager granted you.You do not write grant code. You describe the app in a manifest file, myapp.json:{ "name": "myapp", "exe": "C:\\Tools\\MyApp\\XRUIOS.MyApp.exe", "capabilities": [ "GetEvents", "AddEvent" ], "events": []
}
XRUIOS.Manager install-app myapp.json
XRUIOS.Manager stop
XRUIOS.Manager start
install-app stores the manifest (encrypted) and approves the app's folder. On the next start the Manager registers app:myapp with a fresh password and grants exactly the two capabilities. Edit the file and install again to change the list; a capability you remove is revoked.Anything you don't grant is denied. That's not an error path to code around - it's the model. A good app doesn't offer actions it wasn't granted.Next: the same client behind a window - <a data-href="4 - A Desktop App" href="3-building/4-a-desktop-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">4 - A Desktop App</a>.

3-building/4-a-desktop-app.html.description:
A desktop app is the <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="3 - A CMD App" data-href="3 - A CMD App" href="3-building/3-a-cmd-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">CMD app</a> with a window on top. The broker client is UI-framework-agnostic, so the rule is simple: the connection lives in one small place, the UI calls it, and no XAML ever talks to a worker. The apps that ship with the XRUIOS are written with OpenSilver (XAML) hosted in a Photino window. This page shows that setup first, because it is the one in the repo, and then the same idea with WPF.Every UI app under src/apps has the same shape:src/apps/XRUIOS.App.&lt;Name&gt;/
├─ XRUIOS.App.&lt;Name&gt;/ the OpenSilver UI (App.xaml, MainPage.xaml)
├─ XRUIOS.App.&lt;Name&gt;.Controls/ this app's custom controls
├─ XRUIOS.App.&lt;Name&gt;.Photino/ the desktop window (the production shell)
├─ XRUIOS.App.&lt;Name&gt;.Browser/ browser host
└─ XRUIOS.App.&lt;Name&gt;.Simulator/ WPF simulator for a fast dev loop
<br>They all reference XRUIOS.Apps.Shared, which holds the broker connection, the widget chrome and the holes (see <a data-href="15 - XRUIOS.Aether and Apps.Shared" href="4-understanding-the-wiring/15-xruios.aether-and-apps.shared.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">15 - XRUIOS.Aether and Apps.Shared</a>). The app project itself references only OpenSilver and XRUIOS.Apps.Shared:&lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="OpenSilver" Version="3.3.3" /&gt; &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\..\XRUIOS.Apps.Shared\XRUIOS.Apps.Shared.csproj" /&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
The Photino project is a few lines. This is the Time app:var window = new PhotinoWindow() .SetTitle("Time") .SetSize(1920, 1080) .SetResizable(true) .Center() .ConfigureOpenSilver&lt;App&gt;() .Load("wwwroot/index.html"); window.WaitForClose();
Fullscreen apps (Calendar, Gallery, ThemeEditor, Time, Weather, WorldEvents) look like this. Widgets (AppLauncher, Clipboard, Keyboard, Notifications, PocketPal, Power, VolumeManager) are small, chromeless and always on top, and their MainPage wraps its content in WidgetChrome.public partial class MainPage : Page
{ public MainPage() { InitializeComponent(); Loaded += async (_, _) =&gt; { // One connection for the whole app. The name must match the app's entry in the Manager. await AppShell.InitializeAsync("XRUIOS.App.Time"); // A hole is one line: it calls the Now capability and parses the reply. string now = await TimeSource.NowAsync(); ActiveCountLine.Text = now; }; }
}
<br>AppShell.InitializeAsync calls WorkerBroker.ConnectAsync, which finds credentials from the environment, a dev creds file, or enrollment, exactly as in <a data-href="3 - A CMD App" href="3-building/3-a-cmd-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">3 - A CMD App</a>. The XAML never sees the broker, never holds a password and never names a worker.Instead of polling, subscribe to events the app was granted. Keep the stream while the page is open and dispose it when the page goes away. Callbacks arrive on a background thread, so hand the work back to the UI thread.using XRUIOS.Apps.Shared;
using XRUIOS.Apps.Shared.Holes;
using XRUIOS.Interfaces; // XruiosEventStream
using XRUIOS.Interpreter; // AlarmInfo, XruiosEventNames private XruiosEventStream? _alarms; Loaded += async (_, _) =&gt;
{ await AppShell.InitializeAsync("XRUIOS.App.Time"); _alarms = EventFeed.On&lt;AlarmInfo&gt;(XruiosEventNames.AlarmFired, alarm =&gt; Dispatcher.BeginInvoke(() =&gt; ShowAlarm(alarm)));
}; Unloaded += async (_, _) =&gt; { if (_alarms != null) await _alarms.DisposeAsync(); };
The Time app is granted Events.AlarmFired in AppCatalog. An app that is not granted an event simply never receives it.
Generate it the way src/apps/README.md describes (dotnet new opensilverapp --usePhotino), then reference XRUIOS.Apps.Shared.
Add a hole to XRUIOS.Apps.Shared/Holes/ if the capability you need has none yet (one small file).
Add the app to AppCatalog.cs with the capabilities and events it uses:
new("XRUIOS.App.MyApp", Ui("MyApp"), new[] { "Now", "ListAlarms" }, new[] { XruiosEventNames.AlarmFired }),
Ui("MyApp") makes the Manager look for the executable XRUIOS.App.MyApp.Photino. Every UI app also receives ThemeChanged so it can repaint.
Build, then approve the app's folder: XRUIOS.Manager baseline XRUIOS.App.MyApp.
Start the Manager and run the app. It enrolls itself.
<br>An app that is not part of this repo skips step 3 and ships a manifest for install-app instead (see <a data-href="8 - Building a XRUIOS App" href="2-permissions/8-building-a-xruios-app.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">8 - Building a XRUIOS App</a>).The same idea works without OpenSilver. Reference XRUIOS.SecureLayer (and EclipseProject.dll) like the CMD app does, and put the connection in a service the view model uses.&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt; &lt;PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;OutputType&gt;WinExe&lt;/OutputType&gt; &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net10.0-windows&lt;/TargetFramework&gt; &lt;UseWPF&gt;true&lt;/UseWPF&gt; &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt; &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt; &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\XRUIOS.SecureLayer\XRUIOS.SecureLayer.csproj" /&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;Reference Include="EclipseProject"&gt;&lt;HintPath&gt;..\..\libs\EclipseProject.dll&lt;/HintPath&gt;&lt;/Reference&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;
The service. The credentials step is the same three-way lookup as the CMD app, ending in enrollment:using XRUIOS.Interfaces; public sealed class XruiosClient
{ private EclipseSecureClient? _mgr; public async Task ConnectAsync() { // Environment variables first; otherwise ask the running Manager. The name is the one in the manifest. string? id = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_ID"); string? psk = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_APP_PSK"); string? addr = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XRUIOS_BROKER_ADDR"); if (id is null || psk is null || addr is null) { var prov = await XruiosEnrollment.EnrollAsync("mydesktopapp"); (id, psk, addr) = (prov.AppId, prov.PskBase64, prov.BrokerAddress); } _mgr = await EclipseSecureClient.ConnectAsync(addr, "mydesktopapp", Convert.FromBase64String(psk), identity: id); } public Task&lt;string&gt; GetDayAsync(string day) =&gt; _mgr!.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("GetEvents", new() { ["day"] = day }); public Task&lt;string&gt; AddAsync(string day, string summary) =&gt; _mgr!.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("AddEvent", new() { ["day"] = day, ["summary"] = summary });
}
The view model binds to it. await brings you back to the UI thread, so the only rule is not to block on the connection:public async Task LoadAsync(string day)
{ string raw = await _client.GetDayAsync(day); // off the UI thread Events.Clear(); foreach (var line in raw.Split(';', StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries)) Events.Add(line.Trim()); // back on it
} Connect once, async, off the constructor. Do the handshake in OnStartup (WPF) or Loaded (OpenSilver), show a "connecting" state, and enable the UI only when it is up. A failed handshake is a normal state ("XRUIOS is locked, log in first"), not a crash. Do not swallow it silently either: show it.
A denied capability is a UI affordance, not a swallowed exception. If the app was not granted DeleteEvent, do not render a delete button. The broker refuses it regardless, but a good app does not offer what it cannot do.
The password comes from the Manager. Do not bake a secret into the app or its settings. Ship the executable with none, and let the Manager hand it over.
<br>Next: untrusted code, same gate - <a data-href="5 - An Aether Mod" href="3-building/5-an-aether-mod.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">5 - An Aether Mod</a>.

3-building/5-an-aether-mod.html.description:
A mod is untrusted code loaded inside a HangarBay / Aether Engine host. It can't open a socket to the broker - CasCore makes System.Net uncallable - so the host holds the permission authority and the mod asks it. The gate is the same XRUIOS.Permissions store the Manager uses for apps; a mod is just another requester id in it. The concepts are in <a data-href="6 - Aether Engine Integration" href="2-permissions/6-aether-engine-integration.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">6 - Aether Engine Integration</a>; this page is how to set the projects up.There are two projects: the host (which enrols and gates mods) and the mod (untrusted, loaded under CasCore).Lives in the Aether Engine solution, targets net8.0 (CasCore + the vendored XRUIOS.Permissions DLLs are net8.0).&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt; &lt;PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt; &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt; &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt; &lt;Nullable&gt;disable&lt;/Nullable&gt; &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="DouglasDwyer.CasCore" Version="0.3.0" /&gt; &lt;!-- Transitive deps of the Pariah store that raw DLL refs don't pull. --&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="Ceras" Version="4.1.7" /&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.4" /&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.NetCore" Version="2.2.1" /&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="Konscious.Security.Cryptography.Argon2" Version="1.3.1" /&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="EasyCompressor.LZ4" Version="2.1.0" /&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="K4os.Compression.LZ4" Version="1.3.8" /&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="Data.HashFunction.Blake3" Version="3.1.2" /&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="System.Data.HashFunction.Interfaces" Version="2.0.0" /&gt; &lt;PackageReference Include="Standart.Hash.xxHash" Version="4.0.5" /&gt; &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\Hangarbay.AetherEngine.CasSandbox\Hangarbay.AetherEngine.CasSandbox.csproj" /&gt; &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\Hangarbay.AetherEngine.Security\Hangarbay.AetherEngine.Security.csproj" /&gt; &lt;Reference Include="PermissionHandler"&gt;&lt;HintPath&gt;..\refs\PermissionHandler.dll&lt;/HintPath&gt;&lt;/Reference&gt; &lt;Reference Include="PariahCybersecurity"&gt;&lt;HintPath&gt;..\refs\PariahCybersecurity.dll&lt;/HintPath&gt;&lt;/Reference&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt; &lt;/Project&gt;
Why each:That last row is the one gotcha - a &lt;Reference HintPath&gt; copies the DLL but not its NuGet dependencies, so the store's transitives must be named explicitly.The mod references nothing except the jail type it calls, and even that is not copied (the host provides it). Targets net8.0.&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt; &lt;PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt; &lt;Nullable&gt;disable&lt;/Nullable&gt; &lt;AssemblyName&gt;MyMod&lt;/AssemblyName&gt; &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt; &lt;ItemGroup&gt; &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\Hangarbay.AetherEngine.Security\Hangarbay.AetherEngine.Security.csproj"&gt; &lt;Private&gt;false&lt;/Private&gt; &lt;/ProjectReference&gt; &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;
Private=false matters: the mod uses SandboxFileSystem's type to compile, but must load the host's copy at run time, not ship its own. Everything else a mod might reach - File, Process, sockets, sensors - it simply doesn't reference, and CasCore makes them uncallable anyway.One ModRegistry over the host's shared store. Each mod is Enrolled by its package id (which becomes its host-assigned ModId) and granted its own capabilities. Grants to one never touch another.// the host's ONE permission authority
var context = new AppPermissionContext(SD(storeDir), SD(permClass), SDk(masterKey));
using var registry = new ModRegistry(handler, context, privateRoot, memoTtl: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)); // each row = "register this as its own app, grant it exactly these"
var mod = registry.Enroll("com.hollvania.calendarwidget"); // host-assigned ModId, never self-reported
await registry.GrantAsync(mod, "fs.read");
await registry.GrantAsync(mod, "fs.write");
await registry.GrantAsync(mod, "fs.read:shared");
await registry.GrantAsync(mod, "fs.write:shared"); // hand it its OWN jail: private dir always; the "shared" root opens only with fs.*:shared granted
var fs = registry.CreateFileSystem(mod, new[] { new SandboxRoot("shared", sharedDir) }); // live check, keyed on the mod's id (memoised so per-frame IO is cheap)
bool ok = registry.IsAllowed(mod, "fs.write:shared");
Signed vs ephemeral: Enroll(packageId) gives a stable id whose grants persist across sessions; EnrollEphemeral(label) gives a fresh id each run, so unsigned code can't bank trust. Filesystem access is itself a permission - the self dir needs fs.read/fs.write, a named root needs fs.read:&lt;root&gt;/fs.write:&lt;root&gt;.Hangarbay.AetherEngine.MultiModProvingGround is exactly this, built and run. It loads the default mod template (HostileMod) three times under three package ids, grants each a different set, and checks the outcomes headless:app private shared escapes
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
com.hollvania.calendarwidget allow allow blocked
com.hollvania.musicviz allow deny blocked
com.evil.dataslurp deny deny blocked
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Same mod code, three registrations. Access follows the grant, not the code.
Same bytes, three identities, three access levels - and every raw File / Process / socket escape blocked by CasCore for all of them, regardless of grants. Run it with dotnet run in that project; it exits non-zero if any expectation fails, so it drops into CI next to the single-mod proving ground.To add a distinct mod, copy the default Mod template to a new project with its own AssemblyName, stage its DLL into the host's mods/ folder (the host's StageMods target does this), and enrol it under its own package id.<br>Next: the fleet self-test - <a data-href="6 - The Diagnostics Harness" href="3-building/6-the-diagnostics-harness.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">6 - The Diagnostics Harness</a>.

4-understanding-the-wiring/2-using-yuuko.description:
Using Yuuko Bindings is very simple at the core.For an example, most usage will look likeGeneral Loop
var bindings = new Bindings.DirectoryManager( Path.Combine( Environment.GetFolderPath( Environment.SpecialFolder.LocalApplicationData ), "XRUIOS", "Bindings" )
); await bindings.LoadBindings(); var project = await bindings.GetOrCreateDirectory( @"D:\XRUIOS\Projects\MyProject"
); Console.WriteLine(project.Uuid); While saving the UUID and not the bare path.General Resolvingvar projectPath = await bindings.GetDirectoryById( project.Uuid
); if (projectPath == null)
{ // Ask user to locate the directory.
}
Binding files are usually directly saved between computers, meaning you aren't doing anything to affect the files beyond essentially copy/
To begin, create a Directory Manager.
var manager = new Bindings.DirectoryManager( Path.Combine( Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.LocalApplicationData), "XRUIOS", "Bindings" )
);
The folder will later look likeBindings/
├── A83F21D9C4B72E11.binding
├── 72B8D91A33F01C42.binding
└── ...Although don't worry, if a manager already exists it will still When your app starts up, use
await manager.LoadBindings(); To create a new binding, simply do string projectPath = @"C:\Projects\MyProjects"; var result = await manager.GetOrCreateDirectory(projectPath); Console.WriteLine($"ID: {result.UUID}"); Console.WriteLine($"Path: {result.REe2solvedPath}");
It is hashed from MachineName]FullPath should look like
ID: D423FDENDVF
Path: D:\Projects\MyProjects
Warning
MachineName is not the default one given but rather the one given by the XRUIOS Manager, from the cooresponding worker. Want to resolve a directory? All you need to do is
string? path = await manager.GetDirectoryByID("D423FDENDVF"); if (path != null)
{ Console.WriteLine($"Path: {path}")
} If the directory exists, it'll return. If there's a previously resolved path, it'll return that.
If neither works, you can get a fallback. The bottom function here will create the path if it doesn't exist.string? path = await manager.GetDirectoryByID(
"D423FDENDVF",
"@C:\NewPath\Here"
);
Let's say you have a directory at "C:\NewPath\Here" but you move the project to "D:\DifferentPath\Here". Our question is simple; how do you move the reference?You simply get the UUID ("D423FDENDVF") and create a new binding likeawait manager.UpdateBinding( "D423FDENDVF", new Bindings.DirectoryResolution( @"E:\Projects\ProjectA", verified: true )
);
Now using GetDirectoryByID will return the new path while the UUID stays the same!You can also create this explicitly with var reference = new Bindings.DirectoryRef( @"D:\Projects\MyProject", Environment.MachineName
); var binding = new Bindings.DirectoryBinding(reference); binding.SetResolution( new Bindings.DirectoryResolution( @"D:\Projects\MyProject", verified: true )
);
But it's not worth it for most cases compared to the already existing methods!
